===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}

A {{liushu|p|nocap=y}} of a [[trident]]-like weapon or tool (note the oracle bone script form {{och-l|*𢦐}}). {{zh-ref|Jao & Zeng (1985, pp. 289–290)}} conjecture about its similarity to a kind of three-bladed polearm found at archaeological sites. Cf. {{och-l|*戈|polearm}}, which is the same weapon but with a single blade instead of three. It was already borrowed for sound as an instance of {{liushu|j|nocap=y}} for the pronoun {{och-l|我|I}} since the oracle bone script.

{{w|Guo Moruo}} considers it to be the original character for {{och-l|錡|id=1|gloss=a kind of war [[axe]] with three blades or teeth}}.

{{glossary|folk etymology|Folk etymology}} considers it to be an ideograph of a [[hand]] ({{och-l|*手}}) holding a [[weapon]] ({{och-l|*戈}}) to protect [[oneself]].

===Etymology===
From {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*ŋa}}. Cognate with {{och-l|吾|id=2}}, {{cog|my|sc=Mymr|ငါ|tr=nga|t=I}}, {{cog|hak|sc=Hani|𠊎|tr=ngài}}. The expected Mandarin pronunciation is ''ě'' (now only found in a few Northern dialects); ''wǒ'' is "a col[loquial] archaism" ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}). 

Schuessler further decomposes 我 ''*ŋâiʔ'' (his reconstruction) into ''*ŋa'' + ''*i'', which he terms an "independent suffix" which also appears in some Tibeto-Burman cognates of this pronoun. DeLancey and Benedict believe that the ''*-i'' here was an "information structure marker". But given how Sagart believes 我 was originally plural ({{zh-ref|Sagart 1999:142}}) and DeLancey discovered that first-person plural compounds of {{m|sit-pro|*ŋa|t=I}} and {{m|sit-pro|*i|t=we}} exist in e.g. Raji and Stau, it is also possible that the ''-iʔ'' element is actually {{m|sit-pro|*i|t=we}} fused onto the singular.

